      "muscleGroup": "Biceps",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Biceps volume sits at only 4% of total volume, roughly half of triceps at 8.4%. For long-term muscle balance around the elbow, bringing biceps volume closer to triceps volume over time is worth considering."
    },
    {
      "muscleGroup": "Legs",
      "severity": "mild",
      "observation": "Legs account for 22.4% of volume and showed the largest strength index drop this period (7 points), suggesting they may be accumulating fatigue faster than the upper body groups. Monitoring leg recovery specifically will be important in the next cycle."
    }
  ],
  "deloadRecommended": true,
  "deloadReason": "The combination of your subjective report (joint aches, feeling run down, worsening sessions) and objective data (strength index regression in every single muscle group, with drops of 4 to 7 points across chest, back, legs, and shoulders) makes a deload clearly warranted. These are the hallmark signs of accumulated systemic fatigue. Continuing at current volume and intensity risks injury and further performance decline. A deload week — typically 40 to 60% of your normal volume at reduced intensity — will allow your joints, connective tissue, and nervous system to recover so your next training block starts from a recovered baseline.",
  "actionItems": [
    "Take a full deload this coming week: keep the same exercises but cut your sets roughly in half (aim for around 3 sets per muscle group) and reduce the weight to around 50 to 60% of your recent working loads. The goal is to move, not to push.",
    "Prioritize sleep and nutrition during the deload — this is when the actual recovery happens. Aim for 7 to 9 hours of sleep per night and make sure you are eating enough protein (around 1.6 to 2.2g per kg of bodyweight, so roughly 115 to 160g per day at your current weight) to support tissue repair.",
    "After the deload, gradually ramp volume back up over the first week rather than jumping straight back to 56 sets. Starting at around 40 sets and building back gives your body a chance to adapt rather than shocking it back into fatigue.",
    "Consider adding a small amount of direct biceps work in future cycles to bring it closer in volume to your triceps training, supporting better balance around the elbow joint over the long term."
  ],
  "contextSummary": "User experienced full-body strength regression across all muscle groups (chest -6, back -6, legs -7, shoulders -4) alongside joint pain and low energy, strongly indicating accumulated fatigue — a deload was recommended before resuming progressive overload."
}
